WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:01.930
- I think if you can be yourself

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:04.316
and all that it is,

00:00:04.316 --> 00:00:06.611
what typically happens is people like you.

00:00:06.611 --> 00:00:08.333
Show me somebody who could take a hit

00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:09.720
and I'll show you a good entrepreneur.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.854
Because you know what I found?

00:00:10.854 --> 00:00:13.276
I found desk space was
just like closet space.

00:00:13.276 --> 00:00:14.769
It never stays empty for long.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:16.674
They told me about the Glenn
Avenue amusement park one day,

00:00:16.674 --> 00:00:18.253
a Tom and Chee amusement park.

00:00:18.253 --> 00:00:19.086
I'm, like, okay.

00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:19.940
They're definitely on dope.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.987
What I've learned to get
myself through business

00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:26.205
and build my business is a new
tape and it goes like this.

00:00:26.205 --> 00:00:27.549
(bleep) you.

00:00:27.549 --> 00:00:29.715
All I wanted to do was get a hold of her

00:00:29.715 --> 00:00:31.440
and let her know we'd
figure something out.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.163
I didn't know what, but I knew
we'd figure something out.

00:00:33.163 --> 00:00:33.996
Don't worry.

00:00:33.996 --> 00:00:35.596
That competitive spirit drives you

00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:37.552
through all the things that don't make

00:00:37.552 --> 00:00:38.847
any common sense in life.

00:00:38.847 --> 00:00:40.076
When you're young, you're too stupid

00:00:40.076 --> 00:00:41.143
to know any better.

00:00:41.143 --> 00:00:42.616
You've never jumped off a cliff

00:00:42.616 --> 00:00:43.647
and fallen flat on your face.

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:45.587
If you could work as a free slave, even,

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:48.574
for a licensed top salesperson

00:00:48.574 --> 00:00:49.676
just sitting next to them

00:00:49.676 --> 00:00:51.170
being their slave or servant,

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.804
you're going to learn everything.

00:00:52.804 --> 00:00:55.111
The most underestimated
tool used in business today

00:00:55.111 --> 00:00:56.909
is the phone card.

00:00:56.909 --> 00:00:59.276
- She's an American
businesswoman, investor,

00:00:59.276 --> 00:01:01.393
author, and television personality.

00:01:01.393 --> 00:01:05.036
She's one of the investors
on ABC's hit show Shark Tank.

00:01:05.036 --> 00:01:07.723
She has an estimated net
worth of $80 million.

00:01:07.723 --> 00:01:09.857
She's Barbara Corcoran and here are her

00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:11.940
top 10 rules for success.

00:01:14.565 --> 00:01:16.575
- The upside, when you're
getting much older,

00:01:16.575 --> 00:01:19.848
and I've gotten stronger
and stronger in this way

00:01:19.848 --> 00:01:21.924
by the year, by the minute, okay,

00:01:21.924 --> 00:01:24.682
and I wish I knew it
earlier, is to be yourself

00:01:24.682 --> 00:01:27.208
because people, even if you're weird,

00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:30.584
a weirdo's often the most
loved person in a team.

00:01:30.584 --> 00:01:34.261
I think if you can be
yourself and all that it is,

00:01:34.261 --> 00:01:37.270
what typically happens is people like you

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.937
because they can sense it's the real deal.

00:01:39.937 --> 00:01:43.116
It's the airs, the phoniness,
the trying to impress,

00:01:43.116 --> 00:01:45.162
the insecurity, all the
stuff that we all strive

00:01:45.162 --> 00:01:47.312
so hard for when we're
young that gets in the way.

00:01:47.312 --> 00:01:49.836
There was actually a two year period

00:01:49.836 --> 00:01:51.721
where I had my hair poofed up

00:01:51.721 --> 00:01:53.001
and didn't wash it all week

00:01:53.001 --> 00:01:55.012
to try to fit in with the
society ladies, you know,

00:01:55.012 --> 00:01:57.110
but my scalp itched like crazy.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.578
Had to get rid of that haircut, you know.

00:01:58.578 --> 00:01:59.738
That poof thing once a week.

00:01:59.738 --> 00:02:00.571
Hello.

00:02:01.839 --> 00:02:04.023
(audience laughing)

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.138
And the only time my
business ever fell behind

00:02:06.138 --> 00:02:08.823
was when I was so busy
watching my competition.

00:02:08.823 --> 00:02:10.086
I analyzed everything.

00:02:10.086 --> 00:02:12.541
I had the listings on a chart by category,

00:02:12.541 --> 00:02:14.354
by size, who had what, who's my tar--

00:02:14.354 --> 00:02:15.403
Da, da, da.

00:02:15.403 --> 00:02:17.781
I was, like, a genius at my field.

00:02:17.781 --> 00:02:19.332
It was the only year of my whole career

00:02:19.332 --> 00:02:20.912
I never pushed the business ahead.

00:02:20.912 --> 00:02:22.161
I was so busy watching the next guy,

00:02:22.161 --> 00:02:23.848
I wasn't watching me.

00:02:23.848 --> 00:02:25.427
Being yourself, I would say, would be

00:02:25.427 --> 00:02:26.653
the most important thing.

00:02:26.653 --> 00:02:28.912
Let me tell you, rejection
is the big kahuna

00:02:28.912 --> 00:02:30.368
in all entrepreneurship.

00:02:30.368 --> 00:02:32.076
Show me somebody who can take a hit

00:02:32.076 --> 00:02:34.281
and I'll show you a good entrepreneur.

00:02:34.281 --> 00:02:35.667
I don't know if I made that up

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:36.983
but I think I did.

00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:40.400
But it's all in how well you get back up.

00:02:41.768 --> 00:02:43.297
And you know what I
have found, by the way?

00:02:43.297 --> 00:02:44.861
Because if you think about it,

00:02:44.861 --> 00:02:47.313
great sales people make a
couple million dollars a year

00:02:47.313 --> 00:02:49.463
selling apartments in New York City

00:02:49.463 --> 00:02:50.955
which my great salespeople do.

00:02:50.955 --> 00:02:52.790
Great entrepreneurs.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.637
All business comes from them.

00:02:54.637 --> 00:02:55.954
They manage their own business.

00:02:55.954 --> 00:02:57.021
They hire staff.

00:02:57.021 --> 00:02:58.015
They're entrepreneurs

00:02:58.015 --> 00:02:59.099
and you know what I found

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:00.292
is the only difference

00:03:00.292 --> 00:03:02.390
between the super stars
making the millions a year

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.290
and the person that's barely getting by?

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.695
The only difference, not contacts,

00:03:05.695 --> 00:03:07.866
not how hard they work, some people work

00:03:07.866 --> 00:03:09.730
just as hard and don't
make any money, all right.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.310
It's how long they feel
sorry for themselves

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.946
once they get hit.

00:03:13.946 --> 00:03:16.541
Some people could take a hit

00:03:16.541 --> 00:03:18.050
and milk it for two weeks.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.401
They're at their desk.

00:03:19.401 --> 00:03:21.285
They look like they're working
but they're not working.

00:03:21.285 --> 00:03:22.301
Mentally they're not there.

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:24.023
The great salespeople take the hit

00:03:24.023 --> 00:03:26.583
and then they jump right back up

00:03:26.583 --> 00:03:29.803
as though they've been hurt
just as much as the next guy

00:03:29.803 --> 00:03:31.937
but they work right
through it and keep going.

00:03:31.937 --> 00:03:33.928
Let me tell you, if you have that skill

00:03:33.928 --> 00:03:35.223
you should be an entrepreneur

00:03:35.223 --> 00:03:36.838
because you will be paid well for it.

00:03:36.838 --> 00:03:38.493
The single best thing I do,

00:03:38.493 --> 00:03:40.077
more than anything else,

00:03:40.077 --> 00:03:41.872
I can do in building businesses

00:03:41.872 --> 00:03:43.492
for myself and these other people

00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:45.590
was, just speaking for myself,

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.525
is I fail well.

00:03:47.525 --> 00:03:49.765
If I fall on my face, let me tell you

00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:52.432
nobody's better at pretending I'm not,

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:54.245
thinking of a quick cover up,

00:03:54.245 --> 00:03:55.881
trying to keep moving,

00:03:55.881 --> 00:03:57.303
and you want to know?

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:59.436
In that act itself of
getting back up fast,

00:03:59.436 --> 00:04:02.298
you always stumble onto something good

00:04:02.298 --> 00:04:04.076
and you want to know one other thing?

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.300
I'm not a religious person

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:06.903
'cause I was raised so
god-damned religious.

00:04:06.903 --> 00:04:08.488
I don't want to ever think about it again.

00:04:08.488 --> 00:04:10.987
But I do believe once you put your mind

00:04:10.987 --> 00:04:12.341
on going forward on anything,

00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:14.405
there's almost like a battle cry

00:04:14.405 --> 00:04:15.897
that goes out in the universe

00:04:15.897 --> 00:04:18.230
and the world rallies around you.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:19.328
- You're right.

00:04:19.328 --> 00:04:21.924
- It just comes to your
fore again and again.

00:04:21.924 --> 00:04:23.539
So to have that kind of good faith

00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:24.626
that I'm just getting back up

00:04:24.626 --> 00:04:25.657
even if I'm going to get slammed.

00:04:25.657 --> 00:04:28.288
Life is fair in its own weird way

00:04:28.288 --> 00:04:29.888
in that way of building businesses.

00:04:29.888 --> 00:04:31.966
You will be swept up by some lucky chance.

00:04:31.966 --> 00:04:34.617
It happened to me every
time I got back up.

00:04:34.617 --> 00:04:36.537
Expand before you're ready.

00:04:36.537 --> 00:04:38.970
Mom always said it was never
a good time to have a baby

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.198
and she had 10.

00:04:40.198 --> 00:04:42.439
(audience laughing)

00:04:42.439 --> 00:04:44.288
I don't ever remember
her not being pregnant.

00:04:44.288 --> 00:04:45.177
She was always pregnant.

00:04:45.177 --> 00:04:46.386
In my memory, pregnant.

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:47.859
She was always pregnant, all right.

00:04:47.859 --> 00:04:50.546
I practice the same philosophy
with building my business.

00:04:50.546 --> 00:04:53.013
I found it was never really
a good time to expand

00:04:53.013 --> 00:04:55.255
but I always expanded.

00:04:55.255 --> 00:04:57.906
And the thing that I practice religiously

00:04:57.906 --> 00:04:59.303
that helped me grow my business,

00:04:59.303 --> 00:05:00.822
'cause you have to realize
you're the little guy

00:05:00.822 --> 00:05:02.491
and you've got the big guy up here.

00:05:02.491 --> 00:05:03.858
You can't do the same.

00:05:03.858 --> 00:05:06.170
You've got to do, like,
five times more to catch up.

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:08.146
So what I practice, if I
had a sales organization

00:05:08.146 --> 00:05:10.969
of 25 people and I thought, you know what,

00:05:10.969 --> 00:05:14.475
I'm going to hire an
office that can hold 50.

00:05:14.475 --> 00:05:15.664
I'm going to double the size.

00:05:15.664 --> 00:05:17.152
I never hired the office

00:05:17.152 --> 00:05:19.184
or rented the office that needed 50.

00:05:19.184 --> 00:05:22.262
I went out and killed
myself to get an office

00:05:22.262 --> 00:05:23.415
that held 100.

00:05:23.415 --> 00:05:26.244
I doubled my expectation
because you know what I found?

00:05:26.244 --> 00:05:29.300
I found desk space was
just like closet space.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.076
It never stays empty for long.

00:05:31.076 --> 00:05:33.083
You get all those desks
waiting for bodies.

00:05:33.083 --> 00:05:35.966
Your whole focus is on
finding salespeople.

00:05:35.966 --> 00:05:38.064
Your whole focus is on
training 'em fast and better

00:05:38.064 --> 00:05:40.730
to get 'em productive and
I found that I was able

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.133
to leapfrog over my competitors

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:45.598
because I was super
aggressive in expanding

00:05:45.598 --> 00:05:47.115
before I was ready and I trusted

00:05:47.115 --> 00:05:49.086
that somehow it would all work out

00:05:49.086 --> 00:05:50.173
and it always did work out

00:05:50.173 --> 00:05:51.538
because you have a gun to your own head

00:05:51.538 --> 00:05:52.641
and you have to run like crazy

00:05:52.641 --> 00:05:54.013
to catch up to yourself.

00:05:54.013 --> 00:05:56.431
So it took a little courage
or stupidity on the front side

00:05:56.431 --> 00:05:58.564
but without it I could have never grown

00:05:58.564 --> 00:06:00.484
and become the number
one firm in New York.

00:06:00.484 --> 00:06:01.373
It just wouldn't have happened.

00:06:01.373 --> 00:06:02.526
It was too long in catching up.

00:06:02.526 --> 00:06:04.146
Everybody was in business much too long

00:06:04.146 --> 00:06:05.436
for me to ever catch up.

00:06:05.436 --> 00:06:07.416
So that lesson is expand
before you're ready.

00:06:07.416 --> 00:06:10.455
When I met Tom and Chee,
they had just come out

00:06:10.455 --> 00:06:13.086
of selling their grilled cheese on donuts

00:06:13.086 --> 00:06:14.703
out of a tent in Cincinnati.

00:06:14.703 --> 00:06:17.764
What was so interesting
about them, however,

00:06:17.764 --> 00:06:19.328
they weren't talking about a tent.

00:06:19.328 --> 00:06:20.800
They were talking about a franchise

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.749
of hundreds of franchise
selling cheese on donuts.

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:24.749
Okay, right?

00:06:25.598 --> 00:06:27.673
What do you think happened?

00:06:27.673 --> 00:06:30.271
They told me about the Glenn
Avenue amusement park one day.

00:06:30.271 --> 00:06:31.852
A Tom and Chee amusement park.

00:06:31.852 --> 00:06:33.562
I'm like, okay, they're
definitely on dope.

00:06:33.562 --> 00:06:35.604
(audience laughing)

00:06:35.604 --> 00:06:37.473
But I don't think so any more.

00:06:37.473 --> 00:06:38.982
I can't wait to go to the amusement park.

00:06:38.982 --> 00:06:41.293
They have 171 franchise across America,

00:06:41.293 --> 00:06:43.711
exactly what they envisioned they got.

00:06:43.711 --> 00:06:45.345
Exactly the picture, in great detail,

00:06:45.345 --> 00:06:47.285
where they want to do it,
how they're going to go

00:06:47.285 --> 00:06:48.826
to the other side of
the river in Cincinnati

00:06:48.826 --> 00:06:50.537
which is another state,
I forget which one it is,

00:06:50.537 --> 00:06:52.833
how they're going to come
cross back, buh, buh, buh, buh.

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:54.267
I'm like, okay.

00:06:54.267 --> 00:06:55.962
Well guess what, I should
have believed them.

00:06:55.962 --> 00:06:58.962
They have big pictures in their head

00:06:59.838 --> 00:07:01.189
and every entrepreneur might not have

00:07:01.189 --> 00:07:02.662
a business plan with everything,

00:07:02.662 --> 00:07:04.069
in fact none of them really do,

00:07:04.069 --> 00:07:05.399
I think they're grossly overrated,

00:07:05.399 --> 00:07:07.411
but they sure have a movie in their head

00:07:07.411 --> 00:07:09.204
of who they're going to
be when they grow up.

00:07:09.204 --> 00:07:11.393
And when they can't express
that movie on Shark Tank,

00:07:11.393 --> 00:07:12.424
I'm always out.

00:07:12.424 --> 00:07:15.802
If they don't see it, how am I
going to see it and believe it?

00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:17.168
And they're not going to get there.

00:07:17.168 --> 00:07:20.174
And if I even walk into, like,
a cocktail party or something

00:07:20.174 --> 00:07:22.131
and I meet someone and then it's

00:07:22.131 --> 00:07:24.312
like they're into each other

00:07:24.312 --> 00:07:26.091
and they go, "Nice to meet
you," and they turn away

00:07:26.091 --> 00:07:29.989
that little quarter turn,
I'm embarrassed to admit it,

00:07:29.989 --> 00:07:32.031
I'm, like, dying.

00:07:32.031 --> 00:07:34.662
It's like, oh shit, they don't like me.

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:37.349
Oh my god, you know, this
isn't my crowd anyway.

00:07:37.349 --> 00:07:38.395
I shouldn't have come.

00:07:38.395 --> 00:07:40.904
But what I've learned to
get myself through business

00:07:40.904 --> 00:07:45.059
and build my business is a new
tape and it goes like this.

00:07:45.059 --> 00:07:46.735
(bleep) you.

00:07:46.735 --> 00:07:49.735
(audience laughing)

00:07:51.251 --> 00:07:53.594
I have just as much
right to be here as you.

00:07:53.594 --> 00:07:54.964
I'm just as smart as you are.

00:07:54.964 --> 00:07:57.046
You might not think I'm
smart, but I know I'm smart.

00:07:57.046 --> 00:07:58.860
Guess what, I've done a lot.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.153
Don't you dare look down on me.

00:08:00.153 --> 00:08:01.328
Buh, buh, buh, buh.

00:08:01.328 --> 00:08:02.925
I do this whole thing on myself.

00:08:02.925 --> 00:08:05.260
And you know what it gets me to do?

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.393
It gets me to step forward one more time

00:08:07.393 --> 00:08:09.489
and get just a little bit more conference

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:11.126
with that circumstance,

00:08:11.126 --> 00:08:13.343
but I'm so grateful that I've had

00:08:13.343 --> 00:08:15.053
to come out of that hole to do it.

00:08:15.053 --> 00:08:17.933
Carrie Chiang had, I would
say, 90% of the business

00:08:17.933 --> 00:08:20.388
coming out of Taiwan
'cause she spoke Taiwanese

00:08:20.388 --> 00:08:23.151
and she sold condominiums.

00:08:23.151 --> 00:08:25.248
And then one day I'm
watching the news early

00:08:25.248 --> 00:08:27.189
and I see the Taiwanese stock market,

00:08:27.189 --> 00:08:28.556
if there's such a thing,

00:08:28.556 --> 00:08:30.288
some big Taiwanese thing crashed.

00:08:30.288 --> 00:08:31.567
I'm sitting there.

00:08:31.567 --> 00:08:33.397
I'm like oh my god.

00:08:33.397 --> 00:08:36.260
Carrie is going to be over here.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.324
She was producing single handedly,

00:08:38.324 --> 00:08:41.041
I must have had maybe 150
salespeople at the time,

00:08:41.041 --> 00:08:44.155
she was single handedly
producing 80% of my income.

00:08:44.155 --> 00:08:45.577
This was important, you know.

00:08:45.577 --> 00:08:47.248
All my eggs were in the Carrie basket.

00:08:47.248 --> 00:08:50.056
I immediately called Carrie
up on her first cell phone,

00:08:50.056 --> 00:08:52.617
her second cell phone,
her home, her office,

00:08:52.617 --> 00:08:53.577
her Chinese cousins.

00:08:53.577 --> 00:08:54.801
I could not reach her.

00:08:54.801 --> 00:08:55.761
Next day I can't reach her.

00:08:55.761 --> 00:08:56.635
She's disappeared.

00:08:56.635 --> 00:08:58.555
I know Carrie has committed hara-kiri.

00:08:58.555 --> 00:09:01.222
All I wanted to do was get a hold of her

00:09:01.222 --> 00:09:03.067
to let her know we'd figure something out.

00:09:03.067 --> 00:09:04.935
I didn't know what, but I knew
we'd figure something out.

00:09:04.935 --> 00:09:05.808
Don't worry.

00:09:05.808 --> 00:09:07.871
She finally marches into my office

00:09:07.871 --> 00:09:09.718
as she always does, like she owns it,

00:09:09.718 --> 00:09:11.391
which always insulted me a little

00:09:11.391 --> 00:09:12.848
but what the heck, she's making money.

00:09:12.848 --> 00:09:13.879
I'll settle.

00:09:13.879 --> 00:09:15.173
Marches into my office.

00:09:15.173 --> 00:09:16.582
I go Carrie!

00:09:16.582 --> 00:09:18.517
I wanted to say you're
alive, but I didn't.

00:09:18.517 --> 00:09:19.477
I thought she was dead.

00:09:19.477 --> 00:09:21.844
She goes (speaking foreign
language), you know what I do?

00:09:21.844 --> 00:09:23.761
No Carrie, what did you do?

00:09:23.761 --> 00:09:25.011
I up all night.

00:09:26.467 --> 00:09:29.864
I call everyone of my customers.

00:09:29.864 --> 00:09:32.599
I say you got to sell,
you got to sell now.

00:09:32.599 --> 00:09:34.182
I got 132 listings.

00:09:36.015 --> 00:09:39.015
(audience laughing)

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.375
What do I have to say to that?

00:09:42.375 --> 00:09:43.922
Poetry in motion in sales.

00:09:43.922 --> 00:09:46.559
Can Carrie take a hit?

00:09:46.559 --> 00:09:49.613
She didn't let a hair grow
under her foot, or a grass hair

00:09:49.613 --> 00:09:50.771
or whatever those things are called.

00:09:50.771 --> 00:09:52.122
Bang!

00:09:52.122 --> 00:09:53.522
She saw the opportunity

00:09:53.522 --> 00:09:55.358
and what do you think Carrie did?

00:09:55.358 --> 00:09:58.537
She hired two Japanese speaking assistants

00:09:58.537 --> 00:10:00.264
within, I'd say a week and a half.

00:10:00.264 --> 00:10:01.595
How did she even find them?

00:10:01.595 --> 00:10:04.881
Because the Japanese market was booming

00:10:04.881 --> 00:10:07.479
and she had them as the inter layer.

00:10:07.479 --> 00:10:09.829
She showed apartments to the Japanese

00:10:09.829 --> 00:10:11.871
and let me tell you, I
found out in that chapter

00:10:11.871 --> 00:10:13.648
the Japanese really don't like the Chinese

00:10:13.648 --> 00:10:16.279
and they loved buying
the Chinese apartments

00:10:16.279 --> 00:10:17.628
at a steep discount (laughs).

00:10:17.628 --> 00:10:18.792
(audience laughing)

00:10:18.792 --> 00:10:20.305
And Carrie, like, doubled her business.

00:10:20.305 --> 00:10:23.082
No one sells more property
to the Japanese today

00:10:23.082 --> 00:10:24.128
than Carrie Chiang.

00:10:24.128 --> 00:10:25.407
No one sells more Chinese.

00:10:25.407 --> 00:10:26.790
And if anything happens to those markets,

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.415
I'm sure she'll go to school and learn

00:10:28.415 --> 00:10:30.545
how to speak German,
Russian, whatever the heck

00:10:30.545 --> 00:10:32.325
she has to learn how to do.

00:10:32.325 --> 00:10:35.491
A wonderful example of
someone who takes a hit.

00:10:35.491 --> 00:10:36.412
Competitive.

00:10:36.412 --> 00:10:39.096
If I tell Jim from Cousins Maine Lobster,

00:10:39.096 --> 00:10:40.895
I don't have his picture here but you know

00:10:40.895 --> 00:10:42.246
I talked about him before.

00:10:42.246 --> 00:10:43.598
If I tell him one little thing

00:10:43.598 --> 00:10:45.741
about the guy he used to compete with

00:10:45.741 --> 00:10:49.180
on the hockey team, Jim
was a semi-professional

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:51.242
hockey player and he had some injury

00:10:51.242 --> 00:10:53.585
or something in the minor leagues
if I'm telling that right,

00:10:53.585 --> 00:10:55.949
but anyway, his arch-enemy
on the other team

00:10:55.949 --> 00:10:57.109
was a guy named Luke.

00:10:57.109 --> 00:10:58.135
What do you think happened?

00:10:58.135 --> 00:10:59.008
Luke's from Maine.

00:10:59.008 --> 00:10:59.882
Jim's from Maine.

00:10:59.882 --> 00:11:00.751
Luke went into the lobster business.

00:11:00.751 --> 00:11:03.365
All I have to do to
double sales is say hey,

00:11:03.365 --> 00:11:05.159
did you hear Luke opened
another restaurant?

00:11:05.159 --> 00:11:06.851
(audience laughing)

00:11:06.851 --> 00:11:08.166
Talk about competitive spirit.

00:11:08.166 --> 00:11:09.518
He's ready to kill.

00:11:09.518 --> 00:11:10.813
He doesn't stop working.

00:11:10.813 --> 00:11:12.591
He hates the idea that somebody

00:11:12.591 --> 00:11:14.175
might beat him at a game.

00:11:14.175 --> 00:11:16.536
It's his DNA and his partner,
Steve, is the same way.

00:11:16.536 --> 00:11:18.701
All I have to do is tell Grayson Lace,

00:11:18.701 --> 00:11:21.962
who makes beautiful lacy socks and shawls

00:11:21.962 --> 00:11:24.735
at my most successful business by far

00:11:24.735 --> 00:11:26.122
in terms of making money

00:11:26.122 --> 00:11:27.805
and what do I have to
do to get them going?

00:11:27.805 --> 00:11:28.873
Hardly anything.

00:11:28.873 --> 00:11:30.246
All I have to do is say god,

00:11:30.246 --> 00:11:32.166
got a big check from Cousins today.

00:11:32.166 --> 00:11:33.711
(audience laughing)

00:11:33.711 --> 00:11:35.453
Do you know I get a check from
her, like, within the week.

00:11:35.453 --> 00:11:37.266
A bigger check.

00:11:37.266 --> 00:11:38.140
They're winning?

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.775
We're more successful.

00:11:39.775 --> 00:11:40.964
I didn't say compete.

00:11:40.964 --> 00:11:42.635
That's the way they were.

00:11:42.635 --> 00:11:43.666
Competitive.

00:11:43.666 --> 00:11:45.122
You get an entrepreneur who doesn't

00:11:45.122 --> 00:11:46.282
feel the heat of competition,

00:11:46.282 --> 00:11:48.415
he's not going to be good on his feet,

00:11:48.415 --> 00:11:51.204
she's not going to come to the
fore when she really needs it.

00:11:51.204 --> 00:11:53.535
That competitive spirit drives you through

00:11:53.535 --> 00:11:56.463
all the things that don't
make any common sense in life.

00:11:56.463 --> 00:11:57.458
That's what it does.

00:11:57.458 --> 00:11:58.904
The best time to start your own business

00:11:58.904 --> 00:12:01.144
is as quick as you can and I'm going to

00:12:01.144 --> 00:12:02.495
give you two reasons why.

00:12:02.495 --> 00:12:04.498
When you're young, you're too
stupid to know any better.

00:12:04.498 --> 00:12:06.137
You've never jumped off
a cliff and fallen flat

00:12:06.137 --> 00:12:07.793
on your face so you have courage.

00:12:07.793 --> 00:12:11.328
Once life gets a hold of
you, you learn exactly

00:12:11.328 --> 00:12:14.478
what to be afraid of, what
you might not succeed at.

00:12:14.478 --> 00:12:17.609
Everything gets nipped in
so I think the best time

00:12:17.609 --> 00:12:19.421
to start a business, quite frankly,

00:12:19.421 --> 00:12:21.165
is while you're in college.

00:12:21.165 --> 00:12:22.658
What do you have in college?

00:12:22.658 --> 00:12:24.206
Why is that the perfect time?

00:12:24.206 --> 00:12:25.609
You're open to the universe,

00:12:25.609 --> 00:12:27.849
you have a tremendous support system

00:12:27.849 --> 00:12:30.323
of professors with all
different backgrounds

00:12:30.323 --> 00:12:32.563
ready to help you, and you have colleagues

00:12:32.563 --> 00:12:34.874
with different strengths around you,

00:12:34.874 --> 00:12:36.169
the fellow students,

00:12:36.169 --> 00:12:38.110
and you're hungry.

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:39.888
You're hungry to prove something.

00:12:39.888 --> 00:12:43.692
So, in short, the best
time is the earliest time

00:12:43.692 --> 00:12:45.446
you can possibly do
before you have the wife,

00:12:45.446 --> 00:12:46.750
the kids, the husband, and the mortgage.

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:51.336
And the most fertile time is
right when you're in school,

00:12:51.336 --> 00:12:52.809
even before you get out.

00:12:52.809 --> 00:12:54.713
That's what I believe.

00:12:54.713 --> 00:12:56.030
- But my boyfriend is trying to break

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:57.665
into the real estate industry in New York

00:12:57.665 --> 00:12:59.408
and he's finding it a
little bit challenging

00:12:59.408 --> 00:13:01.126
so I just wanted to know
what advice you would have

00:13:01.126 --> 00:13:02.503
for somebody who's trying to start off.

00:13:02.503 --> 00:13:03.994
- Well, the first thing
you should do is go

00:13:03.994 --> 00:13:05.879
and work for a top salesperson.

00:13:05.879 --> 00:13:06.889
Best way to go in.

00:13:06.889 --> 00:13:08.119
The easiest job to get in New York,

00:13:08.119 --> 00:13:09.576
if you want to go into real estate,

00:13:09.576 --> 00:13:10.729
is to go to a rental company.

00:13:10.729 --> 00:13:13.095
It's like the bottom feeders right there.

00:13:13.095 --> 00:13:15.316
It's terrible, but you
know what's great about it?

00:13:15.316 --> 00:13:17.719
And I ran a rental company for seven years

00:13:17.719 --> 00:13:20.524
before, eight years before
I went to sales, thank god,

00:13:20.524 --> 00:13:22.036
and you know what's great about it?

00:13:22.036 --> 00:13:23.636
You get 100 times at bat.

00:13:23.636 --> 00:13:25.592
In other words, you answer 50 calls.

00:13:25.592 --> 00:13:27.116
Is that apartment available?

00:13:27.116 --> 00:13:28.045
Is that apartment available?

00:13:28.045 --> 00:13:29.486
Different when you're selling.

00:13:29.486 --> 00:13:30.590
It's a slow motion.

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:32.046
So you get a lot of practice in.

00:13:32.046 --> 00:13:34.714
If you could work as a free slave, even,

00:13:34.714 --> 00:13:37.772
for a licensed top salesperson

00:13:37.772 --> 00:13:39.976
just sitting next to them
being their slave or servant

00:13:39.976 --> 00:13:42.074
you're going to learn everything.

00:13:42.074 --> 00:13:43.566
You can't learn in a real estate school.

00:13:43.566 --> 00:13:45.203
You got to be on the street and learn it

00:13:45.203 --> 00:13:46.852
and so that's the most important thing.

00:13:46.852 --> 00:13:48.169
Fun is good for a business.

00:13:48.169 --> 00:13:49.419
Back to my dad.

00:13:50.951 --> 00:13:53.041
I mimic my father's bizarreness

00:13:53.041 --> 00:13:54.991
and it gave me a company culture

00:13:54.991 --> 00:13:56.931
that no one could compete with.

00:13:56.931 --> 00:13:58.884
At first, my competitors made fun of us.

00:13:58.884 --> 00:14:00.543
They called us the Corchettes.

00:14:00.543 --> 00:14:02.072
They said we were like a Midwestern firm,

00:14:02.072 --> 00:14:03.560
which isn't a compliment in New York City.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:06.267
You're supposed to be
sophisticated, you know.

00:14:06.267 --> 00:14:10.100
And so how did I use fun
to build my business?

00:14:12.007 --> 00:14:12.881
Easy.

00:14:12.881 --> 00:14:15.192
The first fun event I
planned when we were, like,

00:14:15.192 --> 00:14:16.328
seven or eight people.

00:14:16.328 --> 00:14:17.513
I think we have a picture of it here

00:14:17.513 --> 00:14:18.834
if you want to go back maybe one slide.

00:14:18.834 --> 00:14:19.758
We'll see it.

00:14:19.758 --> 00:14:20.859
Oh, we're already on to something else.

00:14:20.859 --> 00:14:21.785
All right.

00:14:21.785 --> 00:14:23.761
We got exciting slides
today all over the place.

00:14:23.761 --> 00:14:24.665
Okay.

00:14:24.665 --> 00:14:26.925
But anyway, I'll paint
the picture with my words.

00:14:26.925 --> 00:14:29.199
I told everyone to dress
in your best outfit,

00:14:29.199 --> 00:14:30.674
your absolute best outfit.

00:14:30.674 --> 00:14:32.485
Deck yourself up and
wear your best jewelry,

00:14:32.485 --> 00:14:35.865
do your makeup fresh, not
four o'clock in the afternoon,

00:14:35.865 --> 00:14:37.448
not just wear it from the morning.

00:14:37.448 --> 00:14:38.923
We're going somewhere special.

00:14:38.923 --> 00:14:40.914
I gave them the invitation
two weeks early.

00:14:40.914 --> 00:14:42.392
People talked about it for two weeks.

00:14:42.392 --> 00:14:43.829
Excitement of looking forward to something

00:14:43.829 --> 00:14:47.019
is half the joy of
anything fun, as you know.

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:47.852
What are you wearing?

00:14:47.852 --> 00:14:48.685
What are you wearing?

00:14:48.685 --> 00:14:51.118
They came down to the street
on Madison and 6th Street

00:14:51.118 --> 00:14:53.018
where our office was
and I had an old clunky

00:14:53.018 --> 00:14:55.029
double decker bus waiting for them.

00:14:55.029 --> 00:14:57.210
Looked like it should have
been retired 100 years ago.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:59.133
I said come on up, come on up.

00:14:59.133 --> 00:15:01.089
They sat on the top
'cause it was open aired

00:15:01.089 --> 00:15:03.080
and then I took those fancy ladies

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.940
and one of my salespeople,
a gay guy who dressed

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:07.260
better than all of us.

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:08.185
We couldn't compete.

00:15:08.185 --> 00:15:11.969
We took those fancy
ladies right up to Harlem

00:15:11.969 --> 00:15:13.731
on a slow tour of Harlem when Harlem had

00:15:13.731 --> 00:15:16.398
to be the scariest place in the universe.

00:15:16.398 --> 00:15:17.622
Totally different now.

00:15:17.622 --> 00:15:20.309
They were groping their jewelry.

00:15:20.309 --> 00:15:21.853
They didn't say a word.

00:15:21.853 --> 00:15:25.003
I never saw fancy ladies under pressure.

00:15:25.003 --> 00:15:26.404
They're no good.

00:15:26.404 --> 00:15:28.037
They were terrible.

00:15:28.037 --> 00:15:32.576
I had, thank god, at the time
only one black salesperson,

00:15:32.576 --> 00:15:35.314
V Wilson, with the
biggest soul in the world

00:15:35.314 --> 00:15:36.876
and I cued her where we were going.

00:15:36.876 --> 00:15:39.596
She was my companion here.

00:15:39.596 --> 00:15:42.831
We get up to 145th Street
and Amsterdam Avenue

00:15:42.831 --> 00:15:44.345
and the bus driver leaves.

00:15:44.345 --> 00:15:45.696
Says the engine's broken.

00:15:45.696 --> 00:15:46.600
We planned that.

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:47.900
They leave us for 10 minutes

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:49.016
in the middle of the summer.

00:15:49.016 --> 00:15:50.936
People were nuts.

00:15:50.936 --> 00:15:52.413
(audience laughing)

00:15:52.413 --> 00:15:54.778
Do you know, V Wilson, I mean
we cannot talk about this

00:15:54.778 --> 00:15:57.996
without crying in laughter
like it was yesterday.

00:15:57.996 --> 00:15:59.077
How many years ago was it?

00:15:59.077 --> 00:16:00.558
V Wilson describes that.

00:16:00.558 --> 00:16:04.164
She said when the bus driver left the bus

00:16:04.164 --> 00:16:05.993
she said I swear I was a may--

00:16:05.993 --> 00:16:07.207
What did she say?

00:16:07.207 --> 00:16:08.162
A maypole?

00:16:08.162 --> 00:16:09.660
Like in Sweden, they have maypoles

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:10.915
that kids dance around, I think.

00:16:10.915 --> 00:16:11.951
She said whatever, say flagpole

00:16:11.951 --> 00:16:13.444
but that's not what she said.

00:16:13.444 --> 00:16:15.029
She said I swear I was
a maypole, flagpole,

00:16:15.029 --> 00:16:17.853
with eight white women clinging to me.

00:16:17.853 --> 00:16:19.865
(audience laughing)

00:16:19.865 --> 00:16:22.225
They're like (screams).

00:16:22.225 --> 00:16:23.669
Here's what happened.

00:16:23.669 --> 00:16:24.822
We go back to the office.

00:16:24.822 --> 00:16:28.220
They weren't even talking
to customers the next day.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:30.030
Nobody could talk about anything

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:31.527
but the trip to Harlem.

00:16:31.527 --> 00:16:34.067
They were laughing, sharing
what they were thinking,

00:16:34.067 --> 00:16:35.416
da, da, da, da, da, da, da, da,

00:16:35.416 --> 00:16:38.801
and I looked at my eight salespeople

00:16:38.801 --> 00:16:41.909
and I thought to myself wow, what a team.

00:16:41.909 --> 00:16:43.809
They'd been in trouble together,

00:16:43.809 --> 00:16:46.176
they laughed together,
they're reflecting together,

00:16:46.176 --> 00:16:49.909
and for the first time I
realized I had a culture going

00:16:49.909 --> 00:16:53.229
and so I became the queen of
fun for the rest of my life.

00:16:53.229 --> 00:16:55.527
I probably spent as much
time planning the details

00:16:55.527 --> 00:16:59.987
of ridiculous fun
frequently as much as I did

00:16:59.987 --> 00:17:01.500
with any other part of the business

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.136
'cause I saw it as a power tool

00:17:03.136 --> 00:17:05.340
and guess what I
surprisingly got out of it

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:06.600
that I never expected.

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:10.767
I became the creative, like,
hotbed company in New York.

00:17:11.862 --> 00:17:14.085
All the creative ideas
came out of our company.

00:17:14.085 --> 00:17:15.225
We tried everything first.

00:17:15.225 --> 00:17:16.876
Why did that happen?

00:17:16.876 --> 00:17:18.191
'Cause we thought of all the ideas

00:17:18.191 --> 00:17:19.361
and we're having fun.

00:17:19.361 --> 00:17:21.000
I never had a good idea at my desk.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:23.880
I thought I did, but
the really winning ideas

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.775
always came on the way back
from the movie theater,

00:17:26.775 --> 00:17:30.123
when we were having a management meeting

00:17:30.123 --> 00:17:31.403
and I made everybody sit under the table

00:17:31.403 --> 00:17:33.038
instead of sit at their
chairs just to move it around.

00:17:33.038 --> 00:17:34.902
Anything that was bizarre.

00:17:34.902 --> 00:17:35.989
Get up on an elephant.

00:17:35.989 --> 00:17:38.138
When people are laughing
and laughing and laughing

00:17:38.138 --> 00:17:40.093
they were, you know what
we should really do?

00:17:40.093 --> 00:17:41.231
We should ba, ba, ba, ba.

00:17:41.231 --> 00:17:42.105
People were free.

00:17:42.105 --> 00:17:42.990
We were all equal.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:45.091
We were all playmates and
I created a powerful team

00:17:45.091 --> 00:17:46.707
as a result of fun.

00:17:46.707 --> 00:17:48.840
The most underestimated
tool used in business today

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.148
is the fun part, in my opinion.

00:17:51.148 --> 00:17:52.203
Nobody really has fun.

00:17:52.203 --> 00:17:55.580
People's idea of fun is having a cocktail.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:58.046
Happy New Year in a fancy place.

00:17:58.046 --> 00:17:58.923
It's not fun.

00:17:58.923 --> 00:18:00.416
People do a million things like that.

00:18:00.416 --> 00:18:01.589
I had my February parties

00:18:01.589 --> 00:18:03.557
and what happened at my February parties?

00:18:03.557 --> 00:18:05.441
You weren't allowed in
unless you dressed for it.

00:18:05.441 --> 00:18:06.460
Dress 1940's, okay.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:07.949
How do you dress?

00:18:07.949 --> 00:18:08.964
Figure it out.

00:18:08.964 --> 00:18:10.194
They look forward to it.

00:18:10.194 --> 00:18:11.260
They couldn't get in unless they were--

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:12.342
Dress in pink.

00:18:12.342 --> 00:18:13.287
I'm a guy.

00:18:13.287 --> 00:18:14.454
Dress in pink.

00:18:15.531 --> 00:18:16.364
Cross dress.

00:18:16.364 --> 00:18:17.340
The very last party.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:18.600
Do you know how exciting it was

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.769
to be at the Waldorf Astoria ballroom

00:18:20.769 --> 00:18:24.570
after my straight men
swore they would not come?

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:25.447
We're not coming.

00:18:25.447 --> 00:18:26.280
This is it.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:27.113
You've gone too far.

00:18:27.113 --> 00:18:27.946
I'm not cross dressing.

00:18:27.946 --> 00:18:29.158
I knew they were going to cross dress.

00:18:29.158 --> 00:18:30.736
(audience laughing)

00:18:30.736 --> 00:18:31.827
They didn't want to miss the party.

00:18:31.827 --> 00:18:33.352
They were the best parties in town.

00:18:33.352 --> 00:18:35.809
But do you know how thrilling it was

00:18:35.809 --> 00:18:37.302
for me to be at the Waldorf Astoria,

00:18:37.302 --> 00:18:39.629
their grand ballroom, all that fancy this,

00:18:39.629 --> 00:18:42.707
and see all these people
I couldn't recognize.

00:18:42.707 --> 00:18:43.622
Is that you, Harry?

00:18:43.622 --> 00:18:44.618
It's me, Arna.

00:18:44.618 --> 00:18:46.201
It's me under here.

00:18:47.504 --> 00:18:49.794
It was bizarre, but you want
to know what would happen?

00:18:49.794 --> 00:18:53.527
They would go out and show
listings of the other brokers,

00:18:53.527 --> 00:18:55.625
say you know where we were last night?

00:18:55.625 --> 00:18:56.996
What happened at the party?

00:18:56.996 --> 00:18:58.774
I could have sold every
ticket to my competitors

00:18:58.774 --> 00:19:00.674
for that party for a
thousand a piece if I could,

00:19:00.674 --> 00:19:01.918
if I would have.

00:19:01.918 --> 00:19:04.656
Everybody envied our parties

00:19:04.656 --> 00:19:06.987
'cause they were bizarre, okay,

00:19:06.987 --> 00:19:08.674
and on and on and on.

00:19:08.674 --> 00:19:09.547
I think I told you--

00:19:09.547 --> 00:19:10.875
And by the way, you know what's great

00:19:10.875 --> 00:19:12.070
and still true today in New York.

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:13.580
I'd imagine it's true in
Dallas or wherever you're from.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.998
Do you know that the venues in New York

00:19:15.998 --> 00:19:18.096
are half price in February
'cause it's the worst month

00:19:18.096 --> 00:19:19.096
for parties?

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:21.990
So you get half price, okay.

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:22.860
Tax time.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:24.567
The most depressed sales month of the year

00:19:24.567 --> 00:19:25.811
in every business, February,

00:19:25.811 --> 00:19:27.076
and I don't know why it is but it is

00:19:27.076 --> 00:19:28.265
and it lifts everybody up

00:19:28.265 --> 00:19:30.241
and you get everything at half price.

00:19:30.241 --> 00:19:31.109
Okay.

00:19:31.109 --> 00:19:33.192
Fun is good for business.

00:19:35.623 --> 00:19:36.676
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:19:36.676 --> 00:19:39.343
I made this video because
Victoria Pho asked me to.

00:19:39.343 --> 00:19:40.851
So, if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:19:40.851 --> 00:19:43.411
want me to profile next,
leave it in the comments below

00:19:43.411 --> 00:19:44.641
and I'll see what I can do.

00:19:44.641 --> 00:19:46.679
I'd also love to know which
of Barbara's top 10 rules

00:19:46.679 --> 00:19:48.745
hit you the hardest, is going
to have the biggest impact

00:19:48.745 --> 00:19:50.203
on your business and your life.

00:19:50.203 --> 00:19:51.462
Leave it in the comments

00:19:51.462 --> 00:19:52.747
and I'll join in the discussion.

00:19:52.747 --> 00:19:53.883
Thank you so much for watching.

00:19:53.883 --> 00:19:57.029
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:19:57.029 --> 00:19:59.362
(whooshing)

00:20:02.726 --> 00:20:06.038
- But I was dumping the
pasta one night into the sink

00:20:06.038 --> 00:20:09.664
and all of the sudden
Ramon Simone walks in

00:20:09.664 --> 00:20:11.386
and he says, you know Barbara.

00:20:11.386 --> 00:20:13.342
We have something serious to discuss.

00:20:13.342 --> 00:20:15.238
I'm going to marry your secretary.

00:20:15.238 --> 00:20:16.155
Like, Tina?

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:20.524
She went from Tina the
wonderful secretary.

00:20:20.524 --> 00:20:22.607
I won't even put a label on her.

00:20:22.607 --> 00:20:26.352
I just couldn't believe
my ears or my eyes.

00:20:26.352 --> 00:20:27.805
I'm like what?

00:20:27.805 --> 00:20:28.714
How is that possible?

00:20:28.714 --> 00:20:30.386
He said take your time moving out.

00:20:30.386 --> 00:20:31.610
I took about a minute.

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:35.255
Grabbed a toothbrush
and walked out the door.

00:20:35.255 --> 00:20:36.794
Moved in with my girlfriend Cathy

00:20:36.794 --> 00:20:38.788
who was on East 79th Street in a studio

00:20:38.788 --> 00:20:40.114
and she let me stay there till I got

00:20:40.114 --> 00:20:42.003
my feet back under me.

00:20:42.003 --> 00:20:44.457
I should say that for the
first time in my life,

00:20:44.457 --> 00:20:46.199
I don't know what hit me.

00:20:46.199 --> 00:20:47.159
I guess that hit me.

00:20:47.159 --> 00:20:49.043
But I can't believe I managed it so badly.

00:20:49.043 --> 00:20:50.679
I felt like I was a nobody.

00:20:50.679 --> 00:20:52.938
I went from a somebody
with a successful business

00:20:52.938 --> 00:20:56.886
to a nobody because I was
turned down for a younger woman.

00:20:56.886 --> 00:20:59.283
Tina was five years younger than me.

00:20:59.283 --> 00:21:01.163
I had to admit she was prettier.

00:21:01.163 --> 00:21:03.108
She had real blonde hair.

00:21:03.108 --> 00:21:04.119
I was already highlighting.

00:21:04.119 --> 00:21:05.790
(audience laughing)

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:07.481
I hated her for that.

00:21:07.481 --> 00:21:09.481
She was calm and pretty.

00:21:10.609 --> 00:21:12.439
I hated her.

00:21:12.439 --> 00:21:14.217
(audience laughing)

00:21:14.217 --> 00:21:15.639
But I went to work every day.

00:21:15.639 --> 00:21:18.184
I wanted to fire Tina, but Ray reminded me

00:21:18.184 --> 00:21:19.499
he was the controlling partner.

00:21:19.499 --> 00:21:20.439
I couldn't do that.

00:21:20.439 --> 00:21:22.288
Tina moved into my desk in Ray's office

00:21:22.288 --> 00:21:24.335
where I used to sit and I
sat out with the salespeople

00:21:24.335 --> 00:21:26.661
and every day I went in
smiling like a puppet

00:21:26.661 --> 00:21:28.439
but in my heart I was running around

00:21:28.439 --> 00:21:30.877
broken heart and loss of confidence.

00:21:30.877 --> 00:21:32.908
I just thought to myself,

00:21:32.908 --> 00:21:35.897
my god, I was nothing before Ray found me.

00:21:35.897 --> 00:21:36.997
He picked me up, found me.

00:21:36.997 --> 00:21:38.314
He was my mentor.

00:21:38.314 --> 00:21:39.452
He gave me confidence.

00:21:39.452 --> 00:21:40.959
He gave me the money
to start the business.

00:21:40.959 --> 00:21:42.578
Everything good that had changed my life

00:21:42.578 --> 00:21:45.862
all led to one place,
which was Ramon Simone.

00:21:45.862 --> 00:21:48.092
And I thought, he's right.

00:21:48.092 --> 00:21:49.921
I'll never succeed without him.

00:21:49.921 --> 00:21:52.262
But I can't even remember
what clicked in my head.

00:21:52.262 --> 00:21:53.512
Maybe desperation.

00:21:53.512 --> 00:21:56.551
But one day I just decided I'm
not going to do this anymore

00:21:56.551 --> 00:21:59.094
and I walked in and said to
Ramon Simone, you know what,

00:21:59.094 --> 00:22:01.333
I'm ending this business and
here's how we're going to do it.

00:22:01.333 --> 00:22:03.787
We're going to chop up the 14
salespeople like a football draw.

00:22:03.787 --> 00:22:04.697
You can pick first person.

00:22:04.697 --> 00:22:05.692
I'll pick the second.

00:22:05.692 --> 00:22:06.581
We'll do it fair.

00:22:06.581 --> 00:22:08.003
If you want to move out, you can move out.

00:22:08.003 --> 00:22:09.319
If you want me to move out, no problem.

00:22:09.319 --> 00:22:10.491
You want to keep the phone number?

00:22:10.491 --> 00:22:11.361
No problem.

00:22:11.361 --> 00:22:12.282
I'll get a new phone.

00:22:12.282 --> 00:22:13.511
Whatever.

00:22:13.511 --> 00:22:14.454
You go first.

00:22:14.454 --> 00:22:17.441
He picked Norman Hersh,
our star salesperson.

00:22:17.441 --> 00:22:19.677
I picked Esther Caplan,
our not-so-star salesperson

00:22:19.677 --> 00:22:20.869
but I had a plan for her

00:22:20.869 --> 00:22:22.901
and we went right down the line

00:22:22.901 --> 00:22:25.404
and I would say within maybe six minutes

00:22:25.404 --> 00:22:26.492
we ended a partnership.

00:22:26.492 --> 00:22:27.574
Boom, like that.

00:22:27.574 --> 00:22:29.550
We had $37,000 in cash.

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:32.373
He wrote me a check for half the $37,000

00:22:32.373 --> 00:22:35.715
and as luck would have it, it
was a real estate recession

00:22:35.715 --> 00:22:37.459
we were just about to dive into

00:22:37.459 --> 00:22:38.937
and why was it great?

00:22:38.937 --> 00:22:40.786
Because commercial space
wasn't leasing well.

00:22:40.786 --> 00:22:44.392
He was on the 8th floor
where my old office was.

00:22:44.392 --> 00:22:48.161
I rented the identical space
on the 11th floor above him,

00:22:48.161 --> 00:22:50.399
there's a little ego in
that, I'm sorry to say.

00:22:50.399 --> 00:22:52.839
How needy was I?
(audience laughing)

00:22:52.839 --> 00:22:55.876
And by Monday, this was on a Thursday.

00:22:55.876 --> 00:22:57.939
By Monday I moved my salespeople in

00:22:57.939 --> 00:22:59.820
'cause in those times you
could rent black desks,

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.706
rent phones, bang, we're in operation

00:23:01.706 --> 00:23:03.770
and my seven salespeople moved in

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:05.580
and that was the birth
of the Corcoran Group.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:09.017
Right before I left Ramon
Simone's office that day,

00:23:09.017 --> 00:23:12.003
or I should say Ray and
Tina's office that day,

00:23:12.003 --> 00:23:13.527
and by the way you know
what his real name was?

00:23:13.527 --> 00:23:14.599
It wasn't even Ramon Simone.

00:23:14.599 --> 00:23:16.163
I found out from his
mother, Vicki, his real name

00:23:16.163 --> 00:23:18.952
was Ray Simon and he wasn't
from the best country

00:23:18.952 --> 00:23:20.374
like he always told me.

00:23:20.374 --> 00:23:22.436
He was from 145th Street in Harlem.

00:23:22.436 --> 00:23:23.324
Go figure.

00:23:23.324 --> 00:23:25.221
(audience laughing)

00:23:25.221 --> 00:23:28.242
(laughs) Alrighty, well anyway,

00:23:28.242 --> 00:23:29.930
so right before I left the office

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:32.190
that's when Ray gave me
the gift of a lifetime

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:34.161
when he said to me those words

00:23:34.161 --> 00:23:35.674
that reflected in my
head for the rest of my,

00:23:35.674 --> 00:23:36.970
that still gets me going.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:39.803
"You'll never succeed without me."

00:23:40.932 --> 00:23:43.068
I'm telling you, I don't
believe in negative motivation.

00:23:43.068 --> 00:23:45.168
I'm a positive person like my mother.

00:23:45.168 --> 00:23:47.585
But he really knew my number.

00:23:48.474 --> 00:23:50.019
If he had said I know
you're going to be amazing,

00:23:50.019 --> 00:23:51.546
I'm sure I wouldn't have stayed

00:23:51.546 --> 00:23:53.004
in business in the tough times

00:23:53.004 --> 00:23:56.617
but it was that scolding
tattoo in my heart,

00:23:56.617 --> 00:23:58.516
"You'll never succeed without me,"

00:23:58.516 --> 00:24:00.777
that every time I was near death

00:24:00.777 --> 00:24:02.957
growing my business
through the ups and downs

00:24:02.957 --> 00:24:04.987
of the real estate recession,
being over-extended,

00:24:04.987 --> 00:24:08.259
being over-leveraged, owing
money, blah, blah, blah,

00:24:08.259 --> 00:24:11.443
that same phrase got me going again.

00:24:11.443 --> 00:24:13.861
He gave me an insurance policy for success

00:24:13.861 --> 00:24:15.654
by insulting me.

00:24:15.654 --> 00:24:17.071
Thank God he did.

00:24:18.143 --> 00:24:19.636
I have thanked him a million times.

00:24:19.636 --> 00:24:20.972
What happened to Ramon Simone?

00:24:20.972 --> 00:24:22.146
He married Tina.

00:24:22.146 --> 00:24:23.299
They had three kids together.

00:24:23.299 --> 00:24:24.954
They are so much more happily married

00:24:24.954 --> 00:24:26.854
than myself and my husband, Bill.

00:24:26.854 --> 00:24:27.989
They're meant to be together.

00:24:27.989 --> 00:24:29.841
(audience laughing)

00:24:29.841 --> 00:24:31.674
They were destined, okay.

00:24:31.674 --> 00:24:34.565
But still, thank God
he gave me that insult

00:24:34.565 --> 00:24:37.363
to assure my success
for the rest of my life.

00:24:37.363 --> 00:24:39.426
Every great entrepreneur's a salesman.

00:24:39.426 --> 00:24:41.097
You can't start a business

00:24:41.097 --> 00:24:43.139
if you can't sell your product or service.

00:24:43.139 --> 00:24:45.156
And so if it's not your nature

00:24:45.156 --> 00:24:47.488
to convince somebody to come around

00:24:47.488 --> 00:24:49.565
to your way of thinking,
you're not ever going to

00:24:49.565 --> 00:24:51.236
get your business off the ground.

00:24:51.236 --> 00:24:53.106
Perception creates reality.

00:24:53.106 --> 00:24:54.137
I'm sure you've heard that before

00:24:54.137 --> 00:24:55.870
or another version, a more simple version,

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:58.095
is fake it 'til you make it.

00:24:58.095 --> 00:25:00.678
My mom, when were
raised, was very Catholic

00:25:00.678 --> 00:25:02.703
and she always said remember the meek

00:25:02.703 --> 00:25:03.665
will inherit the earth.

00:25:03.665 --> 00:25:04.998
No need to brag.

00:25:05.928 --> 00:25:06.948
Just be a great person.

00:25:06.948 --> 00:25:08.264
People will recognize it

00:25:08.264 --> 00:25:09.584
and you'll always do well.

00:25:09.584 --> 00:25:10.686
That was my mom's philosophy,

00:25:10.686 --> 00:25:12.268
but she had never been to New York.

00:25:12.268 --> 00:25:13.744
(audience laughing)

00:25:13.744 --> 00:25:14.882
Let me tell you, New York,

00:25:14.882 --> 00:25:16.396
the nice people got mowed over.

00:25:16.396 --> 00:25:18.560
I saw that the first week
that I moved in, all right.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.227
So my mother's philosophy didn't
work in the business world.

00:25:22.227 --> 00:25:23.292
It may have been great for the home

00:25:23.292 --> 00:25:24.965
but it didn't work in the business world.

00:25:24.965 --> 00:25:26.097
You know what I witnessed right away?

00:25:26.097 --> 00:25:30.349
That the big mouth with less
talent inherited the earth.

00:25:30.349 --> 00:25:34.765
And so I had to develop for
myself a really big mouth.

00:25:34.765 --> 00:25:37.089
And I did it very easily

00:25:37.089 --> 00:25:38.811
by creating a rapport

00:25:38.811 --> 00:25:41.782
and I should say my first
perception lesson I learned

00:25:41.782 --> 00:25:44.058
when I got my first $340 rental check,

00:25:44.058 --> 00:25:46.653
I ran over to Bergdorf
Goodman's and blew it.

00:25:46.653 --> 00:25:47.933
I only had a thousand dollars.

00:25:47.933 --> 00:25:50.260
I spent $340 and a thousand, you know.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:53.121
I blew the whole money on the
fanciest coat I could find.

00:25:53.121 --> 00:25:56.072
I bought the fanciest rat haired collar,

00:25:56.072 --> 00:25:58.793
cuffs, buttons, fancy, but you know what.

00:25:58.793 --> 00:26:00.678
I wore that coat for
four years and felt like

00:26:00.678 --> 00:26:02.740
the queen of New York real estate.

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:03.862
Cover up my bad clothes.

00:26:03.862 --> 00:26:05.051
I just sauntered around town

00:26:05.051 --> 00:26:06.509
and you want to know something?

00:26:06.509 --> 00:26:08.784
People responded differently to me

00:26:08.784 --> 00:26:10.012
than when I had my other coat

00:26:10.012 --> 00:26:11.060
that was from New Jersey.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:12.055
They treated me differently

00:26:12.055 --> 00:26:13.249
and I saw the difference

00:26:13.249 --> 00:26:16.038
so I learned to really
create the image of success

00:26:16.038 --> 00:26:17.406
whether I had it or not.

00:26:17.406 --> 00:26:18.278
I was sitting around.

00:26:18.278 --> 00:26:20.944
My salespeople were coming into my office.

00:26:20.944 --> 00:26:23.738
It was another, yet another
real estate recession.

00:26:23.738 --> 00:26:27.221
They were complaining about
me not advertising for them.

00:26:27.221 --> 00:26:28.660
How can we sell if you don't advertise?

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:30.636
Well, I have no money.

00:26:30.636 --> 00:26:32.215
That's how I don't advertise.

00:26:32.215 --> 00:26:33.084
We're out of money.

00:26:33.084 --> 00:26:34.562
We're lucky to have the rent.

00:26:34.562 --> 00:26:35.664
Nah, nah, nah, nah, nah.

00:26:35.664 --> 00:26:36.816
I thought if one more salesperson came

00:26:36.816 --> 00:26:39.403
to my office and complained,
I was going to run away,

00:26:39.403 --> 00:26:40.998
get an illegal gun, and kill 'em.

00:26:40.998 --> 00:26:42.491
(audience laughing)

00:26:42.491 --> 00:26:43.360
That's really how I felt.

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:44.233
I had had it.

00:26:44.233 --> 00:26:46.544
And so, in desperation, I'm thinking okay.

00:26:46.544 --> 00:26:49.724
How do I get people to notice us?

00:26:49.724 --> 00:26:51.380
I thought oh, I know what I'll do.

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:53.386
I took my 11 sales for the year,

00:26:53.386 --> 00:26:55.200
I divided by 11, thank god I was good

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:56.800
at my 11 times tables, the only one,

00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:59.309
came up with an average price

00:26:59.309 --> 00:27:01.371
based on the 11 sales we had for the year

00:27:01.371 --> 00:27:04.124
and I typed on my Selectric typewriter

00:27:04.124 --> 00:27:06.989
the Corcoran report, an
analysis of statistics

00:27:06.989 --> 00:27:08.106
and blah, blah.

00:27:08.106 --> 00:27:09.635
It was so long, I don't
remember what it was.

00:27:09.635 --> 00:27:13.566
But anyway, and I put average
New York City apartment price

00:27:13.566 --> 00:27:16.162
$54,600, whatever it was at the moment,

00:27:16.162 --> 00:27:18.366
and I folded them and mailed them out

00:27:18.366 --> 00:27:20.319
to every reporter that
wrote for the New York Times

00:27:20.319 --> 00:27:21.744
that day, no cover.

00:27:21.744 --> 00:27:23.395
Just a simple one page report.

00:27:23.395 --> 00:27:25.335
One statistic named after me.

00:27:25.335 --> 00:27:26.793
Never got a call.

00:27:26.793 --> 00:27:27.753
Thought hey, big deal.

00:27:27.753 --> 00:27:28.891
Took me a couple of hours.

00:27:28.891 --> 00:27:30.041
That's all it took.

00:27:30.041 --> 00:27:31.431
Hand addressed the envelopes.

00:27:31.431 --> 00:27:33.848
Two weeks hence, I'm at home on a Sunday,

00:27:33.848 --> 00:27:36.002
open up the New York Times, the front page

00:27:36.002 --> 00:27:38.781
of the real estate section which was read

00:27:38.781 --> 00:27:42.364
by everyone, the headline
was New York City

00:27:43.670 --> 00:27:45.420
Prices Hit All Time Low.

00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:46.957
I read the first line.

00:27:46.957 --> 00:27:49.584
It was a Catholic miracle.

00:27:49.584 --> 00:27:50.841
According to Barbara Corcoran,

00:27:50.841 --> 00:27:53.508
president of the Corcoran Group.

00:27:54.581 --> 00:27:56.408
I'm like what?

00:27:56.408 --> 00:27:58.553
(audience laughing)

00:27:58.553 --> 00:27:59.803
I cut that out.

00:28:01.018 --> 00:28:03.427
I went into the office that Monday.

00:28:03.427 --> 00:28:04.569
The world had changed.

00:28:04.569 --> 00:28:05.972
I'm telling you, it
was a bellwether change

00:28:05.972 --> 00:28:07.071
for my business.

00:28:07.071 --> 00:28:09.797
I saw my salespeople hustling on the phone

00:28:09.797 --> 00:28:10.808
trying to get listings.

00:28:10.808 --> 00:28:12.037
You usually called on owner ads.

00:28:12.037 --> 00:28:13.297
Say, can I have your listing?

00:28:13.297 --> 00:28:14.419
There's no MLS.

00:28:14.419 --> 00:28:15.569
Can I have your listing?

00:28:15.569 --> 00:28:17.027
I heard it day in, day out.

00:28:17.027 --> 00:28:18.828
Let me spell it for you.

00:28:18.828 --> 00:28:21.649
C-O-R-C-O-R.

00:28:21.649 --> 00:28:23.036
I always heard my name spelled.

00:28:23.036 --> 00:28:24.090
It was a difficult name

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:25.477
because it repeated itself.

00:28:25.477 --> 00:28:27.092
And that was the first day

00:28:27.092 --> 00:28:29.171
I heard John Beckmann, my German salesman,

00:28:29.171 --> 00:28:31.628
sitting there go, "Oh, you've heard of us?

00:28:31.628 --> 00:28:32.557
"Corcoran?

00:28:32.557 --> 00:28:33.946
"You've heard of us?"

00:28:33.946 --> 00:28:35.581
Everybody stopped.

00:28:35.581 --> 00:28:36.468
He didn't have to spell the name.

00:28:36.468 --> 00:28:38.920
I'm like, that's weird.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:41.717
You know, in people's
minds we went from a nobody

00:28:41.717 --> 00:28:44.988
to a somebody in one day in
the life of New York City

00:28:44.988 --> 00:28:47.155
and I said whoa, I get it.

00:28:48.914 --> 00:28:52.719
I have a partner and it's
called the New York Times.

00:28:52.719 --> 00:28:55.015
And so I churned out the reports.

00:28:55.015 --> 00:28:56.935
If you could advance
the slide a little bit.

00:28:56.935 --> 00:28:58.747
I churned out the report,

00:28:58.747 --> 00:29:01.892
got a new red suit that became our logo.

00:29:01.892 --> 00:29:03.299
You just saw it there quickly.

00:29:03.299 --> 00:29:07.466
And I became the statistician
of numbers in New York.

00:29:10.268 --> 00:29:12.344
I churned out a report on
anything I could think of.

00:29:12.344 --> 00:29:14.230
I churned out a Madonna report

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:16.074
because I read in a post that Madonna

00:29:16.074 --> 00:29:17.643
was looking for an apartment.

00:29:17.643 --> 00:29:18.933
She was pregnant.

00:29:18.933 --> 00:29:20.879
I'm like, we don't have her
but I'll write about her.

00:29:20.879 --> 00:29:22.337
I put out a Madonna report.

00:29:22.337 --> 00:29:23.744
What would Madonna be looking for?

00:29:23.744 --> 00:29:24.683
Top security.

00:29:24.683 --> 00:29:25.674
She was the Lady Gaga of the moment.

00:29:25.674 --> 00:29:27.011
Had to have security.

00:29:27.011 --> 00:29:28.295
A doorman building

00:29:28.295 --> 00:29:29.433
of course, you know.

00:29:29.433 --> 00:29:30.819
She needed the protection.

00:29:30.819 --> 00:29:31.993
Views.

00:29:31.993 --> 00:29:34.338
Of course, anybody with
oodles of money wants views.

00:29:34.338 --> 00:29:36.879
I just took the usual laundry dream list

00:29:36.879 --> 00:29:38.333
that everybody wants when you got money.

00:29:38.333 --> 00:29:42.034
I was on four different
television stations in one week.

00:29:42.034 --> 00:29:42.908
Why?

00:29:42.908 --> 00:29:44.364
Because Madonna was in the news

00:29:44.364 --> 00:29:45.697
and the media was willing to gobble up

00:29:45.697 --> 00:29:47.686
anything that they
could get their hands on

00:29:47.686 --> 00:29:49.036
'cause Madonna was in the news

00:29:49.036 --> 00:29:51.213
and I just happened to provide it.

00:29:51.213 --> 00:29:52.046
All right?

00:29:52.046 --> 00:29:54.184
Do you know no one asked me
if I was working with Madonna.

00:29:54.184 --> 00:29:55.936
(audience laughing)

00:29:55.936 --> 00:29:57.740
You know, the last guy
who introduced me on

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:59.980
the 11 o'clock news, that
stuffy guy that retired

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:03.002
a few years back, I
forget why, some conflict,

00:30:03.002 --> 00:30:06.004
very stuffy, very revered,
he actually introduced me

00:30:06.004 --> 00:30:08.599
without moving his lower lip at all.

00:30:08.599 --> 00:30:10.518
(audience laughing)
Barbara Corcoran,

00:30:10.518 --> 00:30:12.104
celebrity broker.

00:30:12.104 --> 00:30:13.684
I'm like, I'm a celebrity broker?

00:30:13.684 --> 00:30:15.727
(audience laughing)

00:30:15.727 --> 00:30:16.599
Did I get Madonna?

00:30:16.599 --> 00:30:17.473
No.

00:30:17.473 --> 00:30:18.553
Wouldn't that have been nice?

00:30:18.553 --> 00:30:19.500
Actually, she never moved.

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:20.673
She only moved two years ago

00:30:20.673 --> 00:30:23.365
to a double townhouse, but
that's so many years later.

00:30:23.365 --> 00:30:25.061
She never moved, of course,

00:30:25.061 --> 00:30:27.435
but who I did get is a call

00:30:27.435 --> 00:30:29.025
from an agent or an attorney,

00:30:29.025 --> 00:30:30.247
I actually don't know who he was,

00:30:30.247 --> 00:30:33.153
the very next day I was
on that last news channel

00:30:33.153 --> 00:30:35.500
and he said you seem
to be very comfortable

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:36.780
working with celebrities.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:37.811
I wonder if you'd consider

00:30:37.811 --> 00:30:39.319
helping my celebrity find a home.

00:30:39.319 --> 00:30:40.665
Okay.

00:30:40.665 --> 00:30:42.042
(audience laughing)

00:30:42.042 --> 00:30:43.566
Richard Gere.

00:30:43.566 --> 00:30:44.593
Who would you rather have?

00:30:44.593 --> 00:30:47.355
Madonna or Richard Gere?

00:30:47.355 --> 00:30:50.534
(audience laughing)

00:30:50.534 --> 00:30:51.408
That's me.

00:30:51.408 --> 00:30:53.221
I just put my head in her shoes.

00:30:53.221 --> 00:30:56.066
(audience laughing)

00:30:56.066 --> 00:30:57.399
What did he buy?

00:30:58.417 --> 00:30:59.691
Sadly, not something expensive.

00:30:59.691 --> 00:31:02.181
A one bedroom and he ripped the wall down

00:31:02.181 --> 00:31:03.924
and converted it to a temple.

00:31:03.924 --> 00:31:04.990
He was in his temple phase.

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:06.164
Little weird guy.

00:31:06.164 --> 00:31:07.560
(audience laughing)

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:08.973
All right, he resold the apartment, like,

00:31:08.973 --> 00:31:10.498
only three or four years.

00:31:10.498 --> 00:31:11.720
I don't know why he held it

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:13.137
but it was great.

00:31:14.037 --> 00:31:15.601
How did it happen that I got Richard Gere

00:31:15.601 --> 00:31:17.470
and then when we got Richard Gere,

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:19.157
poor Richard Gere, the
minute he bought something

00:31:19.157 --> 00:31:21.361
I did a Richard Gere report.

00:31:21.361 --> 00:31:22.997
Leaked it to the press.

00:31:22.997 --> 00:31:23.974
Richard Gere!

00:31:23.974 --> 00:31:25.413
What's he do?

00:31:25.413 --> 00:31:26.446
It's a temple.

00:31:26.446 --> 00:31:27.604
Don't tell anybody.

00:31:27.604 --> 00:31:29.148
He's making a temple.

00:31:29.148 --> 00:31:30.997
Juicy went on and on and on.

00:31:30.997 --> 00:31:32.937
Richard Gere got me other people.

00:31:32.937 --> 00:31:33.933
They got me other people.

00:31:33.933 --> 00:31:35.014
They got me other people.

00:31:35.014 --> 00:31:35.959
So what came first?

00:31:35.959 --> 00:31:36.828
The chicken or the egg.

00:31:36.828 --> 00:31:38.555
Certainly I created the perception

00:31:38.555 --> 00:31:40.475
and then the reality followed.

00:31:40.475 --> 00:31:42.268
I found I did that my entire life

00:31:42.268 --> 00:31:44.064
with anything I dreamt of becoming.

00:31:44.064 --> 00:31:46.128
I put it in the public eye,

00:31:46.128 --> 00:31:47.586
figured a way to get in the public eye

00:31:47.586 --> 00:31:49.293
and then you know what's great about it?

00:31:49.293 --> 00:31:50.679
You have to run like crazy

00:31:50.679 --> 00:31:52.066
to keep up with yourself.

00:31:52.066 --> 00:31:53.559
You're going to make it happen

00:31:53.559 --> 00:31:55.139
'cause you don't want
to get caught in a lie.

00:31:55.139 --> 00:31:57.506
It's like a dead leg (pants).

00:31:57.506 --> 00:31:59.822
And it worked again and again and again.

